  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">How Much Concrete Do I Need for Fence Posts?</h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Setting fence posts in concrete is the most critical step of building a sturdy fence. If your footings are too small, your fence will lean or blow over in a heavy storm. If you buy too much concrete, you'll be lugging 80-pound bags back to the hardware store.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        The math to figure out exactly how many bags you need involves calculating the volume of your cylindrical hole, and subtracting the volume of the wooden or metal post that sits inside it. Once you know the total empty volume, you can convert it into standard 50lb, 60lb, or 80lb bags of Quikrete.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-blue-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2 mt-0">Skip the Math: Use Our Free Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-800 mb-4">Don't want to calculate Pi and cylindrical volumes by hand? Enter your post size and hole depth into our free tool to instantly get your exact bag count.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/concrete-fence-post" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Go to the Fence Post Concrete Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step-by-Step: How to Calculate Post Hole Concrete</h2>
      
      <p>If you want to understand the volume math and figure it out manually, follow these standard steps.</p>

      <div class="space-y-6 mt-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">1</span>
            Determine Your Hole Dimensions
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">The industry standard rule is that your post hole diameter should be <strong>three times the width of your post</strong>. For a standard 4x4 wooden post (which is actually 3.5 inches wide), your hole should be roughly 10 to 12 inches in diameter. The depth of the hole should be at least 1/3 of the above-ground height of the post, and MUST extend below your local frost line to prevent ground heaving.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">2</span>
            Calculate the Volume of the Hole
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">First, convert all your measurements to inches. The formula for the volume of a cylinder is <code>Pi × r² × height</code>. For example, a 12-inch diameter hole has a radius of 6 inches. If you dig it 36 inches deep, the math is: <code>3.14159 × (6 × 6) × 36 = 4,071.5 cubic inches</code>.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">3</span>
            Subtract the Volume of the Post
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">The concrete won't fill the entire hole because the post is taking up space in the center. You must calculate the volume of the buried portion of the post. A 4x4 post is actually 3.5" x 3.5". If 36 inches of it is buried, the volume is: <code>3.5 × 3.5 × 36 = 441 cubic inches</code>. Subtract this from the hole volume: <code>4,071.5 - 441 = 3,630.5 cubic inches of empty space</code>.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">4</span>
            Convert Cubic Inches to Cubic Yards
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Hardware stores sell concrete based on cubic feet or cubic yards, not cubic inches. To convert cubic inches to cubic yards, divide by 46,656. In our example: <code>3,630.5 / 46,656 = 0.0778 cubic yards per hole</code>.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">5</span>
            Divide by Bag Yield
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Finally, check the yield of the concrete bags you are buying. A standard 50lb bag of fast-setting concrete yields about 0.0138 cubic yards. Divide your required volume by the bag yield: <code>0.0778 / 0.0138 = 5.6 bags</code>. Round up to 6 bags per hole, then multiply by the number of posts in your fence line.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">Do I need gravel at the bottom of a fence post hole?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">Yes, it is highly recommended to add 3 to 4 inches of crushed gravel to the bottom of the hole before inserting the post. This allows water to drain away from the bottom of the wooden post, preventing rot and significantly extending the life of your fence.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">Should I use fast-setting concrete or regular concrete?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">Fast-setting concrete (like Quikrete Fast-Setting in the red bag) is preferred by most DIYers for fence posts because you do not have to mix it in a wheelbarrow. You simply pour the dry mix directly into the hole around the post, and then pour water on top. It sets in 20-40 minutes, meaning you don't have to brace the posts overnight.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">How deep does a fence post need to be?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">As a general rule, one-third to one-half of the above-ground height of the post should be buried. For a 6-foot tall privacy fence, you should dig down at least 2 feet (using an 8-foot post). However, if you live in a cold climate, you MUST dig below the local frost line (often 36 to 48 inches deep) so that freezing groundwater does not push the concrete footing out of the earth.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">How many 50lb bags of fast-setting concrete per 4x4 post?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">For a standard 4x4 post set in a 10-inch diameter hole that is 24 inches deep, you will typically need about three 50lb bags of fast-setting concrete. If your frost line requires a 36-inch hole, you will need four to five 50lb bags per post.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
